---

<CaseStudyOverlay {...toOverlayProps(aiPrototypingKit)}>
  <ProseBlock
    heading="Tools for merchants"
    layout="stacked"
    image={businessHome}
    imageAlt="The Business Home welcome screen, with shortcuts to create content, manage locations, and view analytics, beside “What’s next” cards to turn a single image into a photoshoot and to review brand details"
    imageCaption="Business Home, Google’s next-generation merchant experience."
  >
    <p>
      In 2026, I took a contract with a Google team working on generative-AI
      image tools for merchants. That summer, our team’s main project was to
      launch Business Home, Google’s next-generation merchant experience.
      Business Home aims to bring Google’s suite of tools for merchants and
      small businesses into a single platform, with AI content and photo tools,
      location management, growth guidance, and customer messaging. It launched
      as a <a
        href="https://support.google.com/businesshome/answer/17190673?hl=en"
        >closed beta</a
      > for select businesses in the US and UK.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Prototyping with AI"
    image={aiCodeEditor}
    imageAlt="The Antigravity IDE with the prototyping-kit repository open: its folders in the file explorer, and an agent panel asking “Welcome back, Morgan. Where should we pick up?” above a prompt box set to Gemini 3.8 Flash"
    imageCaption="Google’s AI-coding tool, Antigravity."
  >
    <p>
      At the same time, Google was encouraging designers to adopt its AI code
      editor, known publicly as Antigravity. Designers were experimenting with
      Gemini and Antigravity to build working prototypes alongside their regular
      design work. The promise was speed and higher-fidelity flows for review
      and testing. In practice, it was still messy.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock heading="My role">
    <p>
      I was a UX Designer II, filling in for an L5 Sr designer on the Creative
      Content team, which worked on Google’s Merchant Center. When I first
      started, I owned the “Discovery & Engagement” pillar of Product Studio, a
      new image-generation flow to help users improve the quality and
      consistency of their product-image assets.
    </p>
    <p>
      For Business Home, we were translating Product Studio to an entirely new
      platform and I collaborated with two other designers to recreate the
      primary flows. Over the course of this ~8-week project, I designed and
      built an AI prototyping kit myself, alongside my design work on it. That’s
      how I like to work: constantly improving my process, picking up new tools
      and skills as I go, always pushing what I can produce.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="The first sandbox"
    image={singleHtmlFile}
    imageAlt="A single HTML file with every style packed into its head, beside two supporting files, design context in Markdown and a validation script, and the words “Ballooning file size and token usage…”"
    imageCaption="A single HTML file meant every change touched everything."
  >
    <p>
      Our Design Manager and another Lead UX Designer set out to build a shared
      code sandbox where designers could prototype with AI. When my teammates
      and I tried the first version, we ran into some persistent problems: UI
      did not follow established styles closely, features would randomly change
      or regress, and simple prompts could result in excessively long generation
      turns.
    </p>
    <p>
      When I dug into the sandbox, I realized the problem was likely a lack of
      structure. The sandbox contained a single HTML file with every style,
      view, and function/script embedded. Each request required the agent to
      read the entire prototype. This likely bloated agent context, leading to
      long turns. The lack of compartmentalization also meant a small change or
      hallucination could ripple through the entire prototype and lead to
      unexpected changes.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="A different path"
    image={kitFolders}
    imageAlt="The kit’s four top-level folders: Agents for a consistent workflow, with scripts to review code and utilities for managing the repo; Code for compartmentalized, disposable code that keeps the repo approachable and contains dev minutiae; Docs for UX context as a first-class citizen, with the design system, user context, and content guidelines; and Export for easy sharing of versioned prototypes through an internal hosting tool"
    imageCaption="Separating concerns gave the agent less to read and less to break."
  >
    <p>
      Over the summer the sandbox team kept iterating, focused on centralizing
      the sandbox and syncing it with production code. Those were the right
      goals for them, but as a contractor I did not have access to the monorepo
      where production code lived. So I decided to build my own kit.
    </p>
    <p>
      Though I was building it mainly for myself, I wanted to create something
      other designers could pick up easily—in case I got the chance to
      contribute to the team’s overall process.
    </p>
    <p>
      Years of working with startups and frontend teams, and working on my own
      projects, had given me a working sense of how good codebases are
      organized. I wanted to bring that structure to my kit in order to help
      agents stay on track.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock heading="Starting with a conversation">
    <p>
      I started the way I’d start with an engineering partner: by talking it
      through. I laid out what prototyping needs, above all speed and
      flexibility. I listed the interactions that would need dynamic data or
      services, and asked Gemini to recommend frameworks and an architecture. It
      suggested Vite and Vue for their speed and flexibility.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <AssetRow
    assets={[
      {
        image: vite,
        alt: "The Vite homepage, “The Build Tool for the Web.”",
        caption: "Vite: a fast dev server and build tool.",
      },
      {
        image: vueHomepage,
        alt: "The Vue.js homepage, “The Progressive JavaScript Framework.”",
        caption: "Vue: simple, approachable components.",
      },
      {
        image: vueComponent,
        alt: "Button.vue from this site’s code, opening with a script block that defines the button’s variant, size, and shape props",
        caption: "An example of a Vue component.",
      },
    ]}
  />

  <ProseBlock
    heading="One library, many teams"
    layout="stacked"
    image={guardrails}
    imageAlt="The four guardrails, agents.md, tokens.json, components, and linting, stacked above an abstract burst of horizontal lines, with lines on either side converging toward it"
    imageCaption="Layers of context and guardrails helped lock in the design system."
  >
    <p>
      Many teams were working on different features and flows all at once. A
      team of visual designers was building our own fork of Material Design 3 as
      well. The prototypes coming in from other designers were biased toward
      vanilla M3 components and styles. Because new components and styles were
      not in code format, and because the agent instructions cited M3 as a
      fallback, Gemini would often default to Material Design to fill in gaps.
      The prototypes were functional, and still good for user testing, but it
      was hard to reach high-fidelity final outputs for engineering handoff. I
      realized that, while it is natural to want to give agents as much context
      and tools as possible, sometimes reducing context is helpful. After all,
      AI can create just about anything—the real challenge is creating something
      specific and on-brand.
    </p>
    <p>
      While building mock-ups with the team’s Figma library, I was impressed
      with how sophisticated the library was (and Material Design, from which it
      was forked). There was a complex system of contextual color variables that
      adapted to surface and dark/light modes, as well as extensive type and
      shape/spacing settings. It occurred to me that there was a lot of value
      not being utilized from our Figma library. Moreover, the project was
      highly design-led, meaning that the most up-to-date thinking was usually
      reflected in Figma.
    </p>
    <p>
      With this in mind, I created layers of integration to tie agent context to
      our Figma library and mock-ups. Figma’s design variables were exported as
      JSON tokens the kit could use directly. Agent skills, written from my own
      trial and error, told agents to carefully inspect a frame’s layer
      hierarchy and match its structure in code (to reduce rendering
      discrepancies) and forbade them from using third-party libraries or
      non-token values. I also set up Figma Code Connect to map design
      components to their coded versions, as well as Storybook to spot-check
      component rendering and behavior.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock heading="Hiding the plumbing">
    <p>
      While I am personally comfortable with development tools (terminal, git,
      managing dependencies, running a dev server, etc.), not all designers
      are—and even for me those tools add overhead that can take away from my
      focus. There’s always a tension between a tool’s power and how much you
      are able to focus on the holistic picture. Coding by hand gives you total
      control, but you can easily spend hours on small details and lose a sense
      of how things hang together.
    </p>
    <p>
      I wanted the kit to handle the development busywork so that designers
      could focus more on the overall experience. So I wrote agent skills for
      common workflows: starting and serving the prototype, creating
      experimental branches and carrying updates between them, and exporting a
      build as a zip that could be easily uploaded to an internal sharing tool
      for testing and review.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Putting it to work"
    layout="stacked"
    image={mockData}
    imageAlt="Four kinds of mock data for the prototypes: Users, with persona info as mock user profiles that are switchable in-app; Businesses, by business category, such as retail versus a local restaurant; Assets, context-specific complete sets of images per business, prompted with skill files; and Simulation, with simulated loading and real LLM responses"
    imageCaption="Mock data, dynamically served."
  >
    <p>
      With all of this in place, I used the kit to prototype the flows for our
      upcoming Business Home launch. The difference showed in the details. My
      prototypes used only components and tokens from the new design library,
      creating near-perfect alignment with mock-ups.
    </p>
    <p>
      With the basics handled, I had room to push on interaction and other
      features. I explored ambient effects and special animations for a loading
      interstitial. I shared both with the team as inspiration—though we decided
      not to use them, it was a great example of pushing limits.
    </p>
    <p>
      I also added features to simulate context, including: mock user profiles,
      mock business profiles, custom images for each business, loading timers,
      and more. I even added an API key to feed real LLM responses into a
      simulated AI-assistant feature.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock
    heading="Rebuilding it on my own"
    image={kitReadme}
    imageAlt="The prototyping-kit README on GitHub: “A starting point for designers who prototype with AI agents,” with the kit’s idea, that Code is disposable, Agents evolves, and Docs is the source of truth, and the flow Figma to tokens to prototype and back to Figma"
    imageCaption="The independent kit, rebuilt from the ground up."
  >
    <p>
      When my contract ended, the kit stayed with Google, but what I’d learned
      came with me. I rebuilt the kit from scratch, inspired by my work at
      Google, with no Google code or assets. In the new version I’ve explored
      compatibility with non-Google providers as well as generalizing features
      to work with other design systems and different workflows.
    </p>
    <p>
      The new kit is <a href="https://github.com/morgankeys/prototyping-kit"
        >public on GitHub</a
      >. I used it to <a href="https://github.com/morgankeys/morgankeysdotcomv3"
        >rebuild this portfolio site</a
      >, and I keep refining its skills and tools while I test new workflows and
      configurations. New challenges have arisen and it’s been exciting to
      explore possibilities.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <ProseBlock heading="Impact">
    <p>
      While I wasn’t in a position to steer the shared sandbox, the kit made an
      impression on the people I worked with most closely. Two designers
      directly tried it and came to me for tips and troubleshooting as they
      built their own prototypes. They told others about the kit.
    </p>
    <p>
      My Google point of contact asked me to walk a designer from another team
      through the kit, to help her think through her process. We also showed it
      to our engineers, who were struggling with fidelity and quick turnarounds.
    </p>
    <p>
      At the end of my contract, I handed the kit to my teammates. My point of
      contact even had me share with the broader Business Home team—hopefully
      influencing future sandbox improvements.
    </p>
  </ProseBlock>

  <EndMark logo={logoBadge} label="Google 2026" />
</CaseStudyOverlay>
